WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.346 --> 00:00:06.560
GOPAL ASHOK: Hello.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:07.940
My name is Gopal Ashok.

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:12.050
I'm the Product Manager
for Cloud Memorystore.

00:00:12.050 --> 00:00:15.500
Today we're going to
talk about optimally

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:21.050
deploying an application
cache with Memorystore.

00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:23.440
So let's begin.

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:27.180
So there are a few areas
that I want to cover today.

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:29.810
So I'll start with talking
a little bit about why

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:32.840
it is important to
have an in-memory store

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:34.430
in your application
architecture,

00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:36.392
and what benefits it provides.

00:00:36.392 --> 00:00:37.850
From there, we'll
talk a little bit

00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:41.030
about Redis, and why Redis
is one of the most popular

00:00:41.030 --> 00:00:42.980
in Memorystores to date.

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:46.490
And from there, we will
dive into Memorystore.

00:00:46.490 --> 00:00:48.480
Start with the overview
of the product, what

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:50.480
are some other key features
and key capabilities

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:52.160
that we provide.

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:53.720
But more importantly,
the key thing

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:56.720
that I would like you to take
away from this presentation

00:00:56.720 --> 00:00:59.000
is, what are some of
the best practices

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:03.320
that you should be using when
you are deploying Memorystore

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:04.880
for your application use cases?

00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:11.760
So I think we all
know Memorystores

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:14.760
are a critical part of modern
application architecture.

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:18.240
But let's take a close look
at why that is the case.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:21.960
Without storing data--
or storing and processing

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:24.330
data and memory, it
is impossible to get

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:27.510
the speed that is required
by applications today.

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:31.080
And we all know that the
speed of the applications

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:32.820
and the response time
of the application

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:36.060
essentially determines user
retention, which essentially

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:40.110
means it translates to how
well your business will do.

00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:42.560
So having a Memorystore
that can deliver

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:45.870
the speed that is required
by modern applications

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:48.100
is super-critical.

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:51.210
The other aspect of
it is that speed also,

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:53.730
in a way, or lack of
speed also, in a way,

00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:55.830
translates to availability.

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:58.440
If the website is
super-slow, that essentially

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:00.610
means a website is unavailable.

00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:03.270
So again, a Memorystore
allows you not only just

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:06.430
to have the speed that is
needed by the application,

00:02:06.430 --> 00:02:07.950
but it's also a
critical component

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:09.780
to deliver on the
availability that

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:12.532
is required by the application.

00:02:12.532 --> 00:02:15.520
And the other key aspect where
a Memorystore comes into play,

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:18.650
along with speed and
available, is cost.

00:02:18.650 --> 00:02:20.500
So there are a lot
of use cases where

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:23.200
you can benefit from having
a Memorystore in front

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:25.540
of your database,
or back-end store,

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:30.190
for example, that will allow you
to reduce the total cost when

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:32.030
it comes to data access.

00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:34.660
So by creating a lot more
data from the Memorystore,

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:38.320
you can actually provision,
or downsize, or rightsize

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:40.040
your back-end
store provisioning,

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:44.860
which can help you manage
the overall cost for the data

00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:46.340
access.

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:50.530
So as you can see, a Memorystore
is a super-critical component

00:02:50.530 --> 00:02:54.970
which allows you to do enable
your application to scale,

00:02:54.970 --> 00:02:57.970
and provide the speed
and the responsiveness

00:02:57.970 --> 00:02:59.340
that the application needs.

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:01.390
It allows you to have
better availability,

00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:05.260
and also, it helps
you manage the cost.

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:09.480
So if you take an example,
which is a very common example

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:13.830
of user sessions, or
using-- or a session store,

00:03:13.830 --> 00:03:15.660
typically, what happens
in these scenarios

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:19.100
is that you start off with
using a back-end database

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:20.970
as your session store.

00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:24.120
And for example, let's
say your application

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:26.380
is a ticketing application.

00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:31.730
And if you are using a back-end
store for-- a back-end database

00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:33.860
as your session store,
it'll scale well

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:35.670
for a certain amount of time.

00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:37.370
But once your
application takes off

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:39.482
and you get thousands--
or hundreds of thousands

00:03:39.482 --> 00:03:40.940
of concurrent
connections, you will

00:03:40.940 --> 00:03:44.270
notice that your back-end
store or back-end database,

00:03:44.270 --> 00:03:47.870
for example, post-gres, is
not actually going to scale.

00:03:47.870 --> 00:03:51.530
And this is where having a
Memorystore in the architecture

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:52.450
really, really helps.

00:03:52.450 --> 00:03:55.400
So you can store your
sessions in the Memorystore,

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:57.770
and basically,
because the processing

00:03:57.770 --> 00:03:59.990
time and the processing
is done in-memory,

00:03:59.990 --> 00:04:02.300
and the processing happens
within milliseconds,

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:04.340
your overall latency
for the application

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:06.290
can come down from
hundreds of milliseconds

00:04:06.290 --> 00:04:08.750
in the 90th percentile,
to maybe tens

00:04:08.750 --> 00:04:11.150
of milliseconds towards
the 90th percentile.

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:14.140
So having a Memorystore
for your-- or for scaling

00:04:14.140 --> 00:04:16.160
your application
architectures is

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:19.709
super, super important today.

00:04:19.709 --> 00:04:23.690
So let's take a look at what
are some of the popular choices

00:04:23.690 --> 00:04:26.090
when it comes to a Memorystore.

00:04:26.090 --> 00:04:29.510
I think when you look
at the usage, it is--

00:04:29.510 --> 00:04:32.170
there's no argument that Redis
is the most popular store

00:04:32.170 --> 00:04:33.260
today.

00:04:33.260 --> 00:04:35.210
You can argue most
applications, regardless

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:39.770
of industry or vertical, use
or can benefit from Redis.

00:04:39.770 --> 00:04:42.320
And the popularity is
reflected in dB Engines,

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:45.140
which is a popular website
for rating databases.

00:04:45.140 --> 00:04:48.470
And also in this Stack
Overflow survey, where

00:04:48.470 --> 00:04:50.770
users, developers, and admin--

00:04:50.770 --> 00:04:56.460
dev ops folks rated Redis
as the most-loved database.

00:04:56.460 --> 00:04:58.040
So it's super-clear
that Redis is

00:04:58.040 --> 00:05:02.900
a very, very popular database,
or Memorystore, that is

00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:05.690
used across applications today.

00:05:05.690 --> 00:05:07.730
Why is Redis so popular?

00:05:07.730 --> 00:05:09.860
Redis is an extremely
fancy Memorystore

00:05:09.860 --> 00:05:11.720
that provides
sub-millisecond processing.

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:14.120
But more importantly, it
provides rich data structures

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:18.170
that allow application
developers to do

00:05:18.170 --> 00:05:20.030
fast computations of
applications logic

00:05:20.030 --> 00:05:21.260
on the service side.

00:05:21.260 --> 00:05:24.350
On top of that, Redis
also provides capabilities

00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:26.090
like Pub/Sub and
streaming, which

00:05:26.090 --> 00:05:31.370
allows app developers to build
queuing applications, streaming

00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:34.850
applications, and that
too at a very high scale.

00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:37.640
Apart from that, Redis also
provides high availability

00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:41.690
and scaling features
that allows Redis

00:05:41.690 --> 00:05:46.250
to be used across a wide variety
of use cases at varying scale.

00:05:46.250 --> 00:05:50.750
So altogether, Redis has
become the number 1 choice

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:54.410
for in-memory stores for
building modern application

00:05:54.410 --> 00:05:55.428
architecture.

00:05:58.180 --> 00:06:01.580
So if you're migrating to GCP
or building your applications

00:06:01.580 --> 00:06:05.400
on GCP, Cloud
Memorystore provides you

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:11.100
a fully-managed Redis and
Memcached service on GCP.

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:14.550
So if you are either migrating
your application from on-prem,

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:17.190
or if you are migrating
an application

00:06:17.190 --> 00:06:21.450
from a different cloud, or if
you are managing your own Redis

00:06:21.450 --> 00:06:24.710
on GCE, you now have
a fully-managed Redis

00:06:24.710 --> 00:06:30.490
and Memcached service that you
can use to deploy your Redis

00:06:30.490 --> 00:06:33.310
and Memcached instances.

00:06:33.310 --> 00:06:36.030
I think that the key thing
about having Memorystore

00:06:36.030 --> 00:06:39.300
as a fully-managed service
is that it takes away

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:42.910
the burden of managing the
caching infrastructure.

00:06:42.910 --> 00:06:44.910
So let's take a look at
some of the key features

00:06:44.910 --> 00:06:47.248
and capabilities of Memorystore.

00:06:47.248 --> 00:06:48.790
At the highest level,
as I mentioned,

00:06:48.790 --> 00:06:50.540
Memorystore provides
a fully-managed Redis

00:06:50.540 --> 00:06:52.360
and Memcached service.

00:06:52.360 --> 00:06:56.110
The service is built on
opensource Redis and Memcached,

00:06:56.110 --> 00:06:58.750
which means you can easily lift
and shift your applications

00:06:58.750 --> 00:07:01.000
with very minimum effort.

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:03.640
The other thing
that we enable is

00:07:03.640 --> 00:07:06.040
that maintaining a
caching infrastructure

00:07:06.040 --> 00:07:08.290
is not easy, especially
when you have

00:07:08.290 --> 00:07:11.990
to maintain a highly-available
caching infrastructure.

00:07:11.990 --> 00:07:13.660
So if you were to
do it yourself,

00:07:13.660 --> 00:07:16.090
then you have to
maintain a proxy layer.

00:07:16.090 --> 00:07:17.410
You have to manage all the VMs.

00:07:17.410 --> 00:07:20.050
You have to make sure that
you cache the VMs, et cetera.

00:07:20.050 --> 00:07:22.300
And with Memorystore, we
take care of all that for you

00:07:22.300 --> 00:07:25.120
without our own health checks,
automatic fadeover monitoring,

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:26.080
et cetera.

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:29.050
And on top of that, you get
the Google-grade security,

00:07:29.050 --> 00:07:31.960
where we ensure that the
infrastructure is deployed

00:07:31.960 --> 00:07:35.470
in a very secure environment,
where we use private IPs,

00:07:35.470 --> 00:07:38.420
and you have restricted
access to specific networks,

00:07:38.420 --> 00:07:39.560
et cetera.

00:07:39.560 --> 00:07:41.740
So overall, with
Memorystore, you

00:07:41.740 --> 00:07:46.420
get a fully-managed service
that is highly-available

00:07:46.420 --> 00:07:48.880
and also highly-secure.

00:07:48.880 --> 00:07:52.000
And with that, you
don't really have

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:54.630
to spend time managing
your Redis and Memcached

00:07:54.630 --> 00:07:55.270
infrastructure.

00:07:55.270 --> 00:07:59.260
You can just focus on
building your applications.

00:07:59.260 --> 00:08:01.890
So let's take a closer look
at some of the capabilities

00:08:01.890 --> 00:08:04.910
that we provide for
Memorystore for Redis.

00:08:04.910 --> 00:08:07.950
So Memorystore for Redis comes
in two flavors, a basic tier

00:08:07.950 --> 00:08:08.850
and a standard tier.

00:08:08.850 --> 00:08:11.490
And the basic tier is a
standalone instance of Redis.

00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:14.130
And standard tier is a
highly-available instance

00:08:14.130 --> 00:08:17.310
of Redis, with a
primary and a replica.

00:08:17.310 --> 00:08:20.670
We support up to 300
gigabytes in data set size.

00:08:20.670 --> 00:08:23.130
But the key thing that
I want you to note here

00:08:23.130 --> 00:08:27.000
is that both the tiers are
essentially single-primary.

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:29.910
So that means we don't support
scale-out architecture yet.

00:08:29.910 --> 00:08:32.679
So the QPS that
Memorystore supports

00:08:32.679 --> 00:08:36.260
is limited to what a
single primary can support.

00:08:36.260 --> 00:08:37.980
So when you architect
your application,

00:08:37.980 --> 00:08:39.938
that is something that
you should keep in mind.

00:08:39.938 --> 00:08:43.049
And I'll talk more about that
in the Best Practices section.

00:08:43.049 --> 00:08:45.700
Apart from that, we
provide a backup.

00:08:45.700 --> 00:08:51.150
So if you're using Memorystore
as a critical or primary store,

00:08:51.150 --> 00:08:52.780
you can take backups
of your data.

00:08:52.780 --> 00:08:54.520
So in case of
catastrophic failure,

00:08:54.520 --> 00:08:56.930
you can restore from the backup.

00:08:56.930 --> 00:08:58.440
And we support
multiple versions,

00:08:58.440 --> 00:09:01.350
including Redis version 5.0.

00:09:01.350 --> 00:09:03.360
We've also been working
on some new features

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:06.280
over the last few months.

00:09:06.280 --> 00:09:08.880
So we have shipped
Redis version 5.0.

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:11.190
But one of the other key
features that we have shipped

00:09:11.190 --> 00:09:13.470
is support for shared VPC.

00:09:13.470 --> 00:09:15.990
So if you are deploying
applications and service

00:09:15.990 --> 00:09:19.860
projects, and you want to deploy
Memorystore using a shared VPC

00:09:19.860 --> 00:09:21.770
network, you can do that today.

00:09:21.770 --> 00:09:24.270
So before this
feature was enabled,

00:09:24.270 --> 00:09:27.060
you were not able to deploy
Memorystore instances

00:09:27.060 --> 00:09:28.210
in service projects.

00:09:28.210 --> 00:09:30.060
You had to deploy
it in post-project.

00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:31.860
But now you will
be able to do that.

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:34.860
One thing to note here is that
this feature today is only

00:09:34.860 --> 00:09:36.810
available as part of G Cloud.

00:09:36.810 --> 00:09:38.560
We are working on the UI.

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:40.433
But if you look into
the documentation,

00:09:40.433 --> 00:09:41.850
you will see the
steps that you'll

00:09:41.850 --> 00:09:46.310
have to go through to enable
shared VPC for Memorystore.

00:09:46.310 --> 00:09:48.090
And lastly, we
also enable support

00:09:48.090 --> 00:09:50.023
for VPC Service Controls.

00:09:50.023 --> 00:09:51.690
So if you're using
VPC Service Controls,

00:09:51.690 --> 00:09:56.100
Memorystore works very
seamlessly with it.

00:09:56.100 --> 00:10:00.805
We are also excited to
announce that we have--

00:10:00.805 --> 00:10:03.490
we are providing
support for Memcached

00:10:03.490 --> 00:10:04.990
as part of Memorystore.

00:10:04.990 --> 00:10:06.490
So what we have
heard from customers

00:10:06.490 --> 00:10:08.500
is that there are a lot
of Memcached deployments

00:10:08.500 --> 00:10:10.012
out there for
legacy applications.

00:10:10.012 --> 00:10:11.470
And even for some
new applications,

00:10:11.470 --> 00:10:13.690
customers would like
to use Memcached.

00:10:13.690 --> 00:10:16.030
And with the support
for Memcached today,

00:10:16.030 --> 00:10:19.480
now you have a fully-management
Memcached instance that

00:10:19.480 --> 00:10:22.080
is available with Memorystore.

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:25.150
The Memcached instance is
a scale-out architecture,

00:10:25.150 --> 00:10:26.740
so we support up to 20 nodes.

00:10:26.740 --> 00:10:28.810
You can scale from
1 to 20 nodes,

00:10:28.810 --> 00:10:31.030
and you can have
individual shots.

00:10:31.030 --> 00:10:35.530
Up to 256 gigs with 32 VCPUs.

00:10:35.530 --> 00:10:37.120
So we brought a
lot of flexibility,

00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:40.270
in terms of shaping your
cluster for the different types

00:10:40.270 --> 00:10:45.412
of application workloads that
you may have for Memcached.

00:10:45.412 --> 00:10:46.870
One of the other
key things that we

00:10:46.870 --> 00:10:49.330
provide as part of the service
is the Discovery Endpoint,

00:10:49.330 --> 00:10:50.950
which allows you to
basically discover

00:10:50.950 --> 00:10:53.290
all the nodes in the
cluster from an application

00:10:53.290 --> 00:10:54.220
perspective.

00:10:54.220 --> 00:10:56.920
So that way, when you're scaling
your clusters in and out,

00:10:56.920 --> 00:11:01.210
you always know what
your topology looks like,

00:11:01.210 --> 00:11:04.540
and you don't have to basically
change your app configurations

00:11:04.540 --> 00:11:06.980
when you scale your
clusters up and down.

00:11:06.980 --> 00:11:09.130
So that's a very
convenient service

00:11:09.130 --> 00:11:11.560
that allows you to
minimize the amount of work

00:11:11.560 --> 00:11:16.480
that you need to do whenever
you do scaling of a Memcached

00:11:16.480 --> 00:11:18.750
cluster.

00:11:18.750 --> 00:11:22.080
So we are super-excited about
enabling Memcached for you.

00:11:22.080 --> 00:11:27.050
We are currently in beta, and
we will be going GA pretty soon.

00:11:27.050 --> 00:11:28.830
OK, so we touched
upon a few things.

00:11:28.830 --> 00:11:30.600
We touched upon why
caching is important.

00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:35.530
We touched upon some of the
benefits of using Redis.

00:11:35.530 --> 00:11:37.500
And we talked about Memorystore.

00:11:37.500 --> 00:11:39.300
But the key thing that
I want to talk about

00:11:39.300 --> 00:11:43.140
for the remaining time that
we have in this presentation

00:11:43.140 --> 00:11:45.630
is about the use
cases and patterns,

00:11:45.630 --> 00:11:47.110
and based on that,
what is the best

00:11:47.110 --> 00:11:49.260
practices that you
should follow when

00:11:49.260 --> 00:11:51.730
you are deploying Memorystore?

00:11:51.730 --> 00:11:54.880
So let's take a look at
the use cases and patterns.

00:11:54.880 --> 00:11:59.610
So the way we look
at use cases is

00:11:59.610 --> 00:12:01.970
when it comes to
Redis, especially,

00:12:01.970 --> 00:12:05.110
Redis supports a wide
variety of use cases.

00:12:05.110 --> 00:12:08.640
So from a simple web content
cache or a database cache,

00:12:08.640 --> 00:12:11.880
to a distributed log,
recommendation engines,

00:12:11.880 --> 00:12:14.713
threat detection, fraud
detection, whatnot.

00:12:14.713 --> 00:12:16.380
So there are a wide
variety of use cases

00:12:16.380 --> 00:12:18.500
that Redis is used for.

00:12:18.500 --> 00:12:20.310
But the key thing
to understand is

00:12:20.310 --> 00:12:24.480
that each of these use cases
maps to four different,

00:12:24.480 --> 00:12:26.880
what I call, "caching patterns."

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:28.470
And the caching
patterns essentially

00:12:28.470 --> 00:12:31.590
determine the behavior
that is expected

00:12:31.590 --> 00:12:36.220
from that caching layer for
that particular use case.

00:12:36.220 --> 00:12:37.677
So we see four different use--

00:12:37.677 --> 00:12:38.385
caching patterns.

00:12:38.385 --> 00:12:41.970
The Ephemeral Cache, the
Capacity Cache, the Transient

00:12:41.970 --> 00:12:44.310
Store, and the Primary Store.

00:12:44.310 --> 00:12:46.470
And I'll talk more about
the characteristics

00:12:46.470 --> 00:12:48.570
of these patterns
in the next slide.

00:12:48.570 --> 00:12:50.450
But along with the
patterns, there

00:12:50.450 --> 00:12:53.190
is also the Workload Pattern,
which essentially determines

00:12:53.190 --> 00:12:57.090
the scale, in terms of
data set size, and the QPS;

00:12:57.090 --> 00:12:59.790
the read-write ratio
for the application when

00:12:59.790 --> 00:13:05.230
it comes to accessing the cache;
and also the payload size.

00:13:05.230 --> 00:13:07.320
So the combination of
the workload patterns,

00:13:07.320 --> 00:13:09.810
along with the caching
patterns, determines

00:13:09.810 --> 00:13:12.390
what type of deployment
you should be doing

00:13:12.390 --> 00:13:14.830
for your particular use case.

00:13:14.830 --> 00:13:18.750
So let's take a look at the
different caching patterns

00:13:18.750 --> 00:13:20.875
and what that really means.

00:13:20.875 --> 00:13:22.500
So Ephemeral Cache,
you can think of it

00:13:22.500 --> 00:13:23.580
as a very simple cache.

00:13:23.580 --> 00:13:26.220
If the cache goes away, you
can re-hydrate the cache

00:13:26.220 --> 00:13:27.300
from a back-end store.

00:13:27.300 --> 00:13:30.330
There's no real impact
to the application,

00:13:30.330 --> 00:13:32.850
except for maybe
a slow perform--

00:13:32.850 --> 00:13:36.330
a small performance
depredation that is not

00:13:36.330 --> 00:13:39.192
significant from an
application perspective.

00:13:39.192 --> 00:13:40.650
And for these kind
of use cases, it

00:13:40.650 --> 00:13:46.140
may be enough to just deploy
a memcache or the basic tier

00:13:46.140 --> 00:13:49.690
of Redis, which is
actually very, very cheap.

00:13:49.690 --> 00:13:52.120
But when it comes to the
Transient Store use case,

00:13:52.120 --> 00:13:53.860
it becomes a little
bit more interesting.

00:13:53.860 --> 00:13:56.370
So you have a use case
like session store

00:13:56.370 --> 00:13:58.740
where you have-- you
want to store the keys

00:13:58.740 --> 00:14:00.450
for a certain amount of time.

00:14:00.450 --> 00:14:03.450
But you want the keys to be
available for that period

00:14:03.450 --> 00:14:05.800
of time, because
if you lose a key,

00:14:05.800 --> 00:14:08.280
then the user
experience suffers.

00:14:08.280 --> 00:14:09.995
Again, it is not catastrophic.

00:14:09.995 --> 00:14:11.370
But again, you
don't-- but again,

00:14:11.370 --> 00:14:14.380
the user experience
can get impacted,

00:14:14.380 --> 00:14:20.010
which can also translate to
bad customer satisfaction,

00:14:20.010 --> 00:14:22.720
and customers probably
leaving your service.

00:14:22.720 --> 00:14:24.360
So you want to
make sure that when

00:14:24.360 --> 00:14:26.790
you have use cases
like session store,

00:14:26.790 --> 00:14:29.210
your keys are highly-available.

00:14:29.210 --> 00:14:31.970
The primary store becomes an
even more interesting use case

00:14:31.970 --> 00:14:34.920
where it is the primary
store that is backing your--

00:14:34.920 --> 00:14:38.120
for example, your
front-end website.

00:14:38.120 --> 00:14:40.520
And that data doesn't
exist anywhere else.

00:14:40.520 --> 00:14:43.550
The data is typically
processed by offline processing

00:14:43.550 --> 00:14:48.080
during the day, and it is
ingested into a Redis instance.

00:14:48.080 --> 00:14:50.240
And that essentially
accesses the primary store

00:14:50.240 --> 00:14:52.890
for the applications using it.

00:14:52.890 --> 00:14:56.480
So it's super-important for that
layer to be highly-available.

00:14:56.480 --> 00:14:58.110
And for that
particular use case,

00:14:58.110 --> 00:15:01.610
it is also important to
ensure that the instance is

00:15:01.610 --> 00:15:02.750
very highly-available.

00:15:02.750 --> 00:15:06.380
But you also want to ensure
that the data is backed up,

00:15:06.380 --> 00:15:08.450
in case there is a
catastrophic failure,

00:15:08.450 --> 00:15:12.620
so that you can recover
using the backup when

00:15:12.620 --> 00:15:14.000
the failure happens.

00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:17.330
And lastly, Capacity
Cache is interesting

00:15:17.330 --> 00:15:21.010
from the perspective of how
customers deploy the cache.

00:15:21.010 --> 00:15:24.140
So one of the key things that
Capacity Cache is used for

00:15:24.140 --> 00:15:27.110
is to basically front a
database and to ensure

00:15:27.110 --> 00:15:31.050
that the back-end store
is not impacted as much.

00:15:31.050 --> 00:15:33.890
So what that means is that when
you deploy your Capacity Cache,

00:15:33.890 --> 00:15:37.220
you want to split your key space
across a large number of nodes.

00:15:37.220 --> 00:15:39.460
So that even if one
or two nodes fail,

00:15:39.460 --> 00:15:45.950
the impact on the back-end
database is constrained,

00:15:45.950 --> 00:15:48.900
and you don't really get
into the [INAUDIBLE] problem.

00:15:48.900 --> 00:15:51.260
So this is where the
Memcache deployment can help,

00:15:51.260 --> 00:15:54.140
because now you can spread
your data across a large number

00:15:54.140 --> 00:15:58.910
of nodes and minimize the blast
radius if a node goes down.

00:15:58.910 --> 00:16:01.430
So these are some of the
four different caching

00:16:01.430 --> 00:16:03.320
patterns that we see.

00:16:03.320 --> 00:16:06.830
So what I want to talk about
now is if your application use

00:16:06.830 --> 00:16:11.010
case fits into one of
these caching patterns,

00:16:11.010 --> 00:16:12.560
what are some of
the best practices

00:16:12.560 --> 00:16:15.590
that you should be following
to ensure that you're running

00:16:15.590 --> 00:16:19.610
the Memorystore instance
optimally for your application

00:16:19.610 --> 00:16:21.990
workload?

00:16:21.990 --> 00:16:25.200
So the first thing that
I would like you to do

00:16:25.200 --> 00:16:28.830
is to understand and follow
the Memory Management Best

00:16:28.830 --> 00:16:30.150
Practices for Redis.

00:16:30.150 --> 00:16:32.160
One of the things that
Redis is notorious for

00:16:32.160 --> 00:16:33.580
is poor memory management.

00:16:33.580 --> 00:16:35.430
But as a managed
service, we do a lot

00:16:35.430 --> 00:16:37.350
of things
behind-the-scenes to ensure

00:16:37.350 --> 00:16:40.170
that we take care of some
of the inefficiencies,

00:16:40.170 --> 00:16:44.010
in terms of memory management,
that happens in Redis for you.

00:16:44.010 --> 00:16:46.950
But having said that, it
is super-important for you

00:16:46.950 --> 00:16:51.230
to follow the best
practices, and especially

00:16:51.230 --> 00:16:53.430
following-- monitoring
the right metrics

00:16:53.430 --> 00:16:58.740
to ensure that your instance
is running optimally.

00:16:58.740 --> 00:17:01.980
So let's take a look
at some of the metrics

00:17:01.980 --> 00:17:04.520
that you should be monitoring.

00:17:04.520 --> 00:17:06.640
So we have-- there
are four key metrics,

00:17:06.640 --> 00:17:08.800
from our perspective, that
you should be monitoring

00:17:08.800 --> 00:17:12.010
if you are using Memorystore,
when it comes to memory usage.

00:17:12.010 --> 00:17:15.150
The first one is the
system memory usage ratio.

00:17:15.150 --> 00:17:17.990
The system memory
usage ratio basically

00:17:17.990 --> 00:17:20.960
tells you how much
system memory is

00:17:20.960 --> 00:17:23.119
getting used in your instance.

00:17:23.119 --> 00:17:25.310
So system memory
also includes memory

00:17:25.310 --> 00:17:27.869
that is consumed by
memory fragmentation.

00:17:27.869 --> 00:17:31.220
So if your instance
or your workload

00:17:31.220 --> 00:17:33.440
is causing a lot of
memory fragmentation,

00:17:33.440 --> 00:17:35.210
then the system
memory usage ratio

00:17:35.210 --> 00:17:38.630
will tell you whether the
fragmentation is high or not.

00:17:38.630 --> 00:17:42.080
So you absolutely should monitor
system memory usage ratio,

00:17:42.080 --> 00:17:44.345
and ideally, set a
threshold of about 80%,

00:17:44.345 --> 00:17:48.380
so that you can start making--
rightsizing your instances

00:17:48.380 --> 00:17:50.750
once that threshold is hit.

00:17:50.750 --> 00:17:52.280
The other one is
memory usage ratio.

00:17:52.280 --> 00:17:57.500
That basically tells you
whether your deployed instance

00:17:57.500 --> 00:17:59.630
is basically being
used optimally,

00:17:59.630 --> 00:18:01.970
from a data usage perspective.

00:18:01.970 --> 00:18:03.860
The next one is also
very interesting,

00:18:03.860 --> 00:18:06.090
and something that we
introduced recently,

00:18:06.090 --> 00:18:09.150
which is the time and
system memory overload.

00:18:09.150 --> 00:18:10.550
So what happens
behind-the-scenes

00:18:10.550 --> 00:18:12.830
is that we have
put a cap, in terms

00:18:12.830 --> 00:18:16.260
of system memory, beyond which
we start blocking rights.

00:18:16.260 --> 00:18:18.350
So when we do that,
the whole idea

00:18:18.350 --> 00:18:21.650
is that we ensure that there is
enough system memory available,

00:18:21.650 --> 00:18:25.400
and the system will not go into
an unstable state or crash.

00:18:25.400 --> 00:18:27.920
But the experience
that you will get

00:18:27.920 --> 00:18:30.620
when you hit that limit is,
essentially, "out of memory"

00:18:30.620 --> 00:18:33.380
errors on your applications
when you try to do rights.

00:18:33.380 --> 00:18:35.540
So one of the things
that you want to monitor

00:18:35.540 --> 00:18:39.470
is that, is your instance
getting into that condition

00:18:39.470 --> 00:18:40.050
or not?

00:18:40.050 --> 00:18:40.550
Right?

00:18:40.550 --> 00:18:43.490
It could be that there are
some peak workloads where

00:18:43.490 --> 00:18:45.470
you briefly get
into that condition,

00:18:45.470 --> 00:18:49.460
but then the steady
state is actually OK.

00:18:49.460 --> 00:18:51.490
But it's something
to keep monitoring

00:18:51.490 --> 00:18:53.390
which will allow
you to determine

00:18:53.390 --> 00:18:56.820
whether you need to go
rightsize your instance or not.

00:18:56.820 --> 00:18:58.970
And lastly, evictions is
a interesting-- good thing

00:18:58.970 --> 00:19:03.320
to monitor, to ensure that if
you have an eviction policy set

00:19:03.320 --> 00:19:05.240
where you expect the
keys to be evicted,

00:19:05.240 --> 00:19:06.720
you want to monitor that.

00:19:06.720 --> 00:19:08.630
You know, either
the [INAUDIBLE] set,

00:19:08.630 --> 00:19:11.280
or the keys are getting
evicted so that the memory is

00:19:11.280 --> 00:19:12.990
managed optimally.

00:19:12.990 --> 00:19:15.980
So we highly recommend that
if you're using Memorystore,

00:19:15.980 --> 00:19:20.480
you always monitor these
metrics and set thresholds,

00:19:20.480 --> 00:19:25.070
or rather alerts, that will--
can proactively tell you

00:19:25.070 --> 00:19:29.320
whether your instance is
getting into a bad state or not.

00:19:29.320 --> 00:19:34.130
So once you've done the
memory usage best practices

00:19:34.130 --> 00:19:37.100
and the monitoring around that,
the next thing you want to do

00:19:37.100 --> 00:19:39.530
is, based on the
signals that you get,

00:19:39.530 --> 00:19:42.360
you want to be able to
rightsize your instance.

00:19:42.360 --> 00:19:45.950
So like I mentioned earlier,
your system memory usage ratio

00:19:45.950 --> 00:19:51.320
is a very good indicator of how
well your system or instance is

00:19:51.320 --> 00:19:53.940
performing, in terms
of memory consumption.

00:19:53.940 --> 00:19:57.590
So if you go beyond 80%,
what you would want to do

00:19:57.590 --> 00:20:00.870
is you want to rightsize your
instance by lowering the max

00:20:00.870 --> 00:20:04.520
memory flag, which we
have provided as a G cloud

00:20:04.520 --> 00:20:06.740
option, which will
allow you to give

00:20:06.740 --> 00:20:09.750
more overhead for the instance.

00:20:09.750 --> 00:20:11.360
This is super-important,
especially

00:20:11.360 --> 00:20:13.610
when you are doing
things like backup,

00:20:13.610 --> 00:20:16.640
or you are scaling
the instance often.

00:20:16.640 --> 00:20:19.970
Because when you do a backup
or when you scale the instance,

00:20:19.970 --> 00:20:25.910
we do kick-off an RDB process,
which essentially is a child

00:20:25.910 --> 00:20:30.600
process that we spin up that
can consume, even double,

00:20:30.600 --> 00:20:32.510
the memory that you
are using, depending

00:20:32.510 --> 00:20:36.500
on the write rate
on your instance.

00:20:36.500 --> 00:20:40.160
So especially if your instance
is a write-heavy instance,

00:20:40.160 --> 00:20:43.700
we highly recommend that
you provide higher--

00:20:43.700 --> 00:20:46.310
more overhead by
lowering max memory,

00:20:46.310 --> 00:20:49.970
to ensure that operations
like backup and scaling

00:20:49.970 --> 00:20:53.840
can happen successfully.

00:20:53.840 --> 00:20:57.220
So that's about
rightsizing the instance.

00:20:57.220 --> 00:21:00.250
So just to recap, monitor
your memory management

00:21:00.250 --> 00:21:04.780
best practices, and rightsize
based on the thresholds

00:21:04.780 --> 00:21:07.630
that you see for system
memory usage ratio,

00:21:07.630 --> 00:21:13.612
or the metric around time
[INAUDIBLE] memory condition.

00:21:18.540 --> 00:21:23.000
And finally, I just wanted to
touch upon some general best

00:21:23.000 --> 00:21:26.240
practices that you should
be following, especially

00:21:26.240 --> 00:21:28.418
for the primary store use case.

00:21:28.418 --> 00:21:29.960
So one of the things
that I mentioned

00:21:29.960 --> 00:21:33.110
earlier is that the
Memorystore instances

00:21:33.110 --> 00:21:35.750
today, both basic and
standard tier instances,

00:21:35.750 --> 00:21:41.450
are single-primary, meaning
all of your application queries

00:21:41.450 --> 00:21:44.090
are going against
a single primary.

00:21:44.090 --> 00:21:46.160
So what that means
is that you want

00:21:46.160 --> 00:21:48.950
to be able to run your
benchmark for your application

00:21:48.950 --> 00:21:53.500
and understand whether the
QPS, especially the QPS,

00:21:53.500 --> 00:21:55.272
is hitting the limits.

00:21:55.272 --> 00:21:57.230
The other thing I would
say is that if you have

00:21:57.230 --> 00:22:01.800
a right-intensive workload,
meaning if it is--

00:22:01.800 --> 00:22:02.840
80-20 reads.

00:22:02.840 --> 00:22:06.260
What is this, 50-50 read-write?

00:22:06.260 --> 00:22:10.010
You really want to
monitor your memory usage

00:22:10.010 --> 00:22:12.470
and follow the best
practices that I mentioned

00:22:12.470 --> 00:22:13.750
in the previous slide.

00:22:13.750 --> 00:22:16.040
It is super-critical you
do that so that you can

00:22:16.040 --> 00:22:17.722
keep your instance right size.

00:22:17.722 --> 00:22:19.430
And not only that,
you can also determine

00:22:19.430 --> 00:22:21.597
whether you're hitting the
limits of the Memorystore

00:22:21.597 --> 00:22:22.320
instance.

00:22:22.320 --> 00:22:24.695
One of the ways you can hit
the limits of the Memorystore

00:22:24.695 --> 00:22:26.390
instance, and determine
whether you're

00:22:26.390 --> 00:22:28.790
hitting the limits of
the Memorystore instance,

00:22:28.790 --> 00:22:30.900
is by monitoring
the replication.

00:22:30.900 --> 00:22:33.410
So if there's a lot of writes,
and if replication is not

00:22:33.410 --> 00:22:36.020
keeping up, then what
can potentially happen

00:22:36.020 --> 00:22:38.390
is that you are
getting fadeover.

00:22:38.390 --> 00:22:42.080
You can lose some keys that
has not been replicated.

00:22:42.080 --> 00:22:45.650
And you can basically
run into scenarios

00:22:45.650 --> 00:22:47.260
where there's a lot
of key space loss

00:22:47.260 --> 00:22:49.000
if the replication
log is too much.

00:22:49.000 --> 00:22:51.560
So you basically
want to monitor it.

00:22:51.560 --> 00:22:53.842
And lastly, if you're using
it as a primary store,

00:22:53.842 --> 00:22:56.050
you absolutely should take
a backup of your instance.

00:22:56.050 --> 00:23:00.660
We have published a paper on
how to scheduled backups using

00:23:00.660 --> 00:23:01.940
Cloud Scheduler.

00:23:01.940 --> 00:23:05.260
So that is published in
our solution website.

00:23:05.260 --> 00:23:07.760
And we have also
published a solution paper

00:23:07.760 --> 00:23:11.360
around best practices
around performance

00:23:11.360 --> 00:23:13.770
from Memorystore,
Redis in general.

00:23:13.770 --> 00:23:16.500
So I'd highly recommend if
you're using Memorystore today,

00:23:16.500 --> 00:23:18.950
or thinking about using
Memorystore, that you follow

00:23:18.950 --> 00:23:22.850
the best practices around memory
management, and you follow--

00:23:22.850 --> 00:23:25.550
you monitor the metrics
that is recommended.

00:23:25.550 --> 00:23:27.920
And also, learn
how to take actions

00:23:27.920 --> 00:23:30.800
depending on what the
metrics are telling you.

00:23:30.800 --> 00:23:33.920
So by doing that, you are
essentially being proactive

00:23:33.920 --> 00:23:37.730
about ensuring that the
Memorystore instance runs

00:23:37.730 --> 00:23:39.080
in a stable state.

00:23:39.080 --> 00:23:40.970
And it also, more
importantly, allows

00:23:40.970 --> 00:23:44.930
you to determine
whether the Memorystore

00:23:44.930 --> 00:23:49.820
instance is actually
suitable for your workloads.

00:23:49.820 --> 00:23:53.300
In some cases, if you find the
Memorystore instances are not

00:23:53.300 --> 00:23:55.310
able to keep up,
then you will have

00:23:55.310 --> 00:23:58.250
to shard your Memorystore
or application

00:23:58.250 --> 00:24:00.940
workload across multiple
Memorystore instances

00:24:00.940 --> 00:24:03.160
using blindside sharding.

00:24:03.160 --> 00:24:05.710
So that, hopefully, gives
you a general understanding

00:24:05.710 --> 00:24:07.690
on what are some of the
best practices that you

00:24:07.690 --> 00:24:10.210
should be following
when using Memorystore.

00:24:10.210 --> 00:24:12.680
And I hope that
you find it useful.

00:24:12.680 --> 00:24:18.120
And it is something that you can
put into practice immediately.

00:24:18.120 --> 00:24:19.760
So with that, I
just want to touch

00:24:19.760 --> 00:24:23.780
upon some useful
links that will help

00:24:23.780 --> 00:24:27.560
you understand a
lot more about what

00:24:27.560 --> 00:24:30.000
I've just talked about here.

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:32.120
So if you-- there is
a Memory Management

00:24:32.120 --> 00:24:35.375
Best Practices documentation
that we have published.

00:24:35.375 --> 00:24:37.250
We have published the
Performance During Best

00:24:37.250 --> 00:24:40.790
Practices, and we also have
published a solution paper

00:24:40.790 --> 00:24:46.430
on how to scheduled backups
using Cloud Scheduler.

00:24:46.430 --> 00:24:49.360
All this together,
hopefully, will give you

00:24:49.360 --> 00:24:52.070
a great start, in terms of
running your Memorystore

00:24:52.070 --> 00:24:53.750
instance optimally.

00:24:53.750 --> 00:24:55.690
Thank you very much.

